{"name":"Daisy Chain","hex":"#fff09b","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"daisy chain","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A garland to be worn on the head, made (usually as a pastime) by sewing or otherwise linking the stems of the flowers of daisies (Bellis perennis) into a ring.","A thing resembling a daisy chain (noun sense 1) in having several elements connected together, often in a circle."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Alternative form of daisy-chain."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/daisy_chain"}],"entries":[{"title":"Garland","blocks":[{"text":"A garland is a decorative braid, knot or wreath of flowers, leaves, or other material. Garlands can be worn on the head or around the neck, hung on an inanimate object, or laid in a place of cultural or religious importance. In contemporary times, garlands are used to decorate, especially around holidays."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Choinka.JPG/330px-Choinka.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garland","wikidata":"Q756600"},{"title":"Daisy chain (electrical engineering)","blocks":[{"text":"In electrical and electronic engineering, a daisy chain is a wiring scheme in which multiple devices are wired together in sequence or in a ring, similar to a garland of daisy flowers. Daisy chains may be used for power, analog signals, digital data, or a combination thereof."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Daisy_chain.svg/330px-Daisy_chain.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daisy_chain_(electrical_engineering)","wikidata":"Q1157702"},{"title":"Chain","blocks":[{"text":"A chain is a serial assembly of connected links typically made of metal, with an overall character similar to that of a rope in that it is flexible and curved in compression, but linear, rigid, and load-bearing in tension. A chain may consist of two or more links. Chains can be classified by their design, which can be dictated by their use:"},{"items":["Those designed for lifting, such as when used with a hoist; for pulling; or for securing, such as with a bicycle lock, have links that are torus-shaped, which make the chain flexible in two dimensions. Small chains serving as jewellery are a mostly decorative analogue of such types.","Those designed for transferring power in machines have links designed to mesh with the teeth of the sprockets of the machine, and are flexible in only one dimension. They are known as roller chains, though there are also non-roller chains such as block chains."]}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Broad_chain_closeup.jpg/330px-Broad_chain_closeup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chain","wikidata":"Q2397485"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Choinka.JPG/500px-Choinka.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Choinka.JPG/1280px-Choinka.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Choinka.JPG/500px-Choinka.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tinsel garlands on a Christmas tree","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChoinka.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Daisy_chain.JPG/500px-Daisy_chain.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Daisy_chain.JPG/1280px-Daisy_chain.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2